  "query_preview": "..(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 355 AM EST THU NOV 5 2026\n\nThe main story for the first part of the extended period will be \nthe cold weather that settles in behind the Friday cold front. A \ntrough over the Great Lakes region will barrel down towards the \nUpper Ohio Valley and Mid-Atlantic states through the early part \nof the weekend. As the 500 mb trough moves east of the area, cold\nair will begin to work in for Saturday and following lows in the\n30s to around 40, daytime highs are not expected to get out of \nthe 50s. Cloud cover will gradually diminish as the upper level \nsystem moves off to the east, which will lead to low temperatures\ntrending colder for Sunday. Strong ridge valley splits are \nexpected for both Saturday and Sunday night as nighttime lows are \nexpected to be in the 30s for ridge tops and the 20s for the more \nsheltered valley locations. A widespread frost and/or freeze \nappears likely for both nights.\n\nHigh pressure will build across the Commonwealth through the early \npart of next week, leading to dry weather and near normal \ntemperatures. Model solutions indicate a more active pattern\ndeveloping around midweek. LREF and ECMWF indicate the potential \nfor troughing developing across the eastern CONUS, while the GFS \nis more aggressive and brings a strong upper-level low into the \nCentral Appalachians vicinity on Thursday. Given the model \nspread, there is still uncertainty with the location and evolution\nof these features. These will continue to be monitored for \npotential precipitation chances as we head into late next week.",
